Bug: rows.Close() not deferred in delete/update returning paths — connection leak on panic

## Describe the feature

Fix resource leaks where `*sql.Rows` are not deferred in the delete and update returning paths, risking connection pool exhaustion if `gorm.Scan` panics.

## Motivation

`callbacks/query.go` and `callbacks/create.go` correctly use `defer rows.Close()`. However, `callbacks/delete.go:173-179` and `callbacks/update.go:89-99` call `rows.Close()` inline without defer. If `gorm.Scan` panics (possible given heavy reflect usage), `rows.Close()` is never called, leaking the underlying database connection. Under panic conditions, this can exhaust the connection pool.

### `callbacks/delete.go:173-179`
```go
if rows, err := db.Statement.ConnPool.QueryContext(...); db.AddError(err) == nil {
    gorm.Scan(rows, db, mode)
    // ...
    db.AddError(rows.Close())  // NOT deferred
}
```

### `callbacks/update.go:89-99`
```go
if ok, mode := hasReturning(db, supportReturning); ok {
    if rows, err := db.Statement.ConnPool.QueryContext(...); db.AddError(err) == nil {
        // no defer rows.Close() here!
        gorm.Scan(rows, db, mode)
        db.AddError(rows.Close())  // NOT deferred
    }
}
```

### Expected pattern (from `callbacks/query.go:24-26`)
```go
if rows, err := db.Statement.ConnPool.QueryContext(...); db.AddError(err) == nil {
    defer func() { db.AddError(rows.Close()) }()
    gorm.Scan(rows, db, mode)
}
```

## Related Issues

- Performance architecture review of GORM codebase

