Cold Conn, EphemeralConn, and Watch paths panic with streaming credentials

With streaming credentials enabled, a dedicated-connection or transaction call can panic when it needs to initialize a fresh connection. An unrecovered panic can terminate the application.

The [derived Conn constructor](https://github.com/redis/go-redis/blob/v9.23.0/redis.go#L3006-L3020) and [transaction constructor](https://github.com/redis/go-redis/blob/v9.23.0/tx.go#L42-L63) do not carry the parent's streaming-credentials manager. When initialization is needed, `initConn` calls `Listener` on that nil manager. An already-initialized pooled connection avoids the dereference.

## Impact

An application using Redis WATCH for optimistic transactions can hit this on its first cold request, even with a healthy Redis server and valid credentials. A warm-up PING can hide the problem until the pool needs another fresh connection. Recovery around request handlers may contain the panic, but the affected operation still fails.

## Reproduction

The test exercises the supported derived-client APIs against a local Redis server. Warm-connection cases are controls; no Azure account is needed.

<details>
<summary>Test and run instructions</summary>

Start a disposable Redis instance in another terminal:

```sh
redis-server --bind 127.0.0.1 --port 16379 --save '' --appendonly no --requirepass old-token
```

In a `go-redis v9.23.0` checkout, save the following as `derived_client_repro_test.go` in the repository root. It uses the existing `mockStreamingProvider` helper.

