Bug: @xstate/solid not updating arrays properly (createImmutable.ts)

### Description

First off thank you for an amazing open sourced library. I know the title is a duplicate of bug #3853 . That was done intentionally as it seems the fix for the issue did work however it only works if the arrays are different sizes or the length is divisible by two. 


### Expected result

Given these two arrays as an example
```javascript
const next = [4,3,2,1,0]
const prev = [1,2,3,4,5]
```
The output should be `[4,3,2,1,0]`

### Actual result

However the current output is 
```javascript
[4,3,3,1,0]
```

 The issue lies in the for loop here 

https://github.com/statelyai/xstate/blob/98f9f9165df328bba68622b69ec46dada0e3a4f7/packages/xstate-solid/src/createImmutable.ts#L56-L59C8

```typescript
      // Diff array
      for (let start = 0, end = largestSize - 1; start < end; start++, end--) {
        diff(next[start], prev[start], [...path, start] as Path);
        diff(next[end], prev[end], [...path, end] as Path);
      }
```

In this loop both `start` and `end` are incremented / decremented on each iteration.  Because of the use of th `<` operator the start and end will miss the center value in an odd length array.  

## Example
```typescript
const next = [4,3,2,1,0]
const prev = [1,2,3,4,5]
for (let start = 0, end = next.length - 1; start < end; start++, end--) {
  console.log(`start: ${start}, next[start]: ${next[start]}, prev[start]: ${prev[start]}`)
  console.log(`end: ${end}, next[end]: ${next[end]}, prev[end]: ${prev[end]}\n`)
  if (next[start] !== prev[start]) prev[start] = next[start]
  if (next[end] !== prev[end]) prev[end] = next[end]
}
console.log(`updated: [${prev}]`)
```

The above code will output the following

```bash
start: 0, next[start]: 4, prev[start]: 1
end: 4, next[end]: 0, prev[end]: 5

start: 1, next[start]: 3, prev[start]: 2
end: 3, next[end]: 1, prev[end]: 4

updated: [4,3,3,1,0]
```

As you can see because `start` and `end` don't converge the 3rd element in the `next` array doesn't get assigned to the updated state. 

One solution would be to change `start < end` to `start < end + 1` then add a conditional equality check between the two diff operations for `start === end` and break the loop.

The other is obviously using a traditional unidirectional for loop. So I thought I would compare the approaches and see which one preformed better.  

```javascript
import { Bench } from 'tinybench';
const bench = new Bench();

function diff1(prev, next) {
  const state = [...prev];
  for (let start = 0, end = next.length - 1; start < end; start++, end--) {
    if (next[start] !== prev[start]) state[start] = next[start]
    if (next[end] !== prev[end]) state[end] = next[end]
  }
  return state;
}

function diff2(prev, next) {
  const state = [...prev];
  for (let start = 0, end = next.length; start < end; start++) {
    if (next[start] !== prev[start]) state[start] = next[start]
  }
  return state;
}

function diff3(prev, next) {
  const state = [...prev];
  for (let start = 0, end = next.length - 1; start < end + 1; start++, end--) {
    if (next[start] !== prev[start]) state[start] = next[start]
    if (start == end) break;
    if (next[end] !== prev[end]) state[end] = next[end]
  }
  return state;
}

function randomPrevAndNext() {
  return [Array.from({length: 5}, () => Math.floor(Math.random() * 100)), Array.from({length: 5}, () => Math.floor(Math.random() * 100))]
}


bench.add("diff fixed multidirection", diff3.bind(null, ...randomPrevAndNext()))
bench.add("diff multidirection broken", diff1.bind(null, ...randomPrevAndNext()))
bench.add("diff unidirection", diff2.bind(null, ...randomPrevAndNext()))
await bench.run();
console.table(bench.table());
```

I used a randomly generated array of length 5 to eliminate any caching or JIT optimizations that would have skewed the results. 

```bash
┌─────────┬──────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────┬──────────┬─────────┐
│ (index) │          Task Name     
